43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "b8dfb9da-35e7-59ee-baf1-bb6dbd054214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334652/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e639c766-c4dc-544b-8f1a-977c32a3759b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334652/field-4/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "ce240398-757e-5bbe-8666-2d8212a44702" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334652/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "cd06c816-ed4d-59eb-92dc-09c8a85a28b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334652/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "42a6359a-27f7-567e-a79f-553a4d10ba82" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334652/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-334652/field-4/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334652/field-4/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-334652/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334652/field-4/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334652/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334652" :question/id #uuid "6acd5ad6-cecd-50bc-9edc-fc2a85e9b7ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-334652" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =5(0.5)^{4}-(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.062\\,5-1\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =5(0.25)^{4}-(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.003\\,90...-1\\cdot 0.000\\,97... \\\\  & =0.018\\,55... \\\\  & ≈0.019.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.018\\,55....$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.018\\,55...}=15.157\\,89…≈15.158.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈15.157\\,89… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.157\\,89…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.922… \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334652/field-1" "q-334652/field-2" "q-334652/field-3" "q-334652/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334652"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334709/field-1/86eb5cd6a25f8b56fa5397d39eb33195eee5e99259f30091422117c37f3ab993" :answer/id #uuid "a953d5aa-5044-5beb-8e46-34fb86d932e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.050" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334709/field-1/1a3be31ce841089d983ce95b41acc0710e9dcb167b519d6db44f5afe5c9dd0c0" :answer/id #uuid "1ff11c9b-c433-5eac-9106-369e77a125f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334709/field-1/be2996522c88880b85c94a2df96123acadb7ac9f8da34e41793102bb8efe9a83" :answer/id #uuid "97703265-295f-526a-9b79-872e4f4c1f17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.075" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334709/field-1/f21d17c56a55341b783f4b794af71be3aae28eaf8e805f8667404b8a657e3c15" :answer/id #uuid "7512c079-1ea5-5bc6-8b3e-96cba61f8e02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.004" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334709/field-1/b34544a9837d181994c7a132937df1564b327d325fab23b2c613d8bcfe8cb599" :answer/id #uuid "4ef7761e-c2fc-5fc2-8142-28d01c1cf622" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334709/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e87f74e6-d992-5bee-ba58-d63d1a5c43ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334709/field-1/1a3be31ce841089d983ce95b41acc0710e9dcb167b519d6db44f5afe5c9dd0c0" :answer-field/choices ["q-334709/field-1/86eb5cd6